69 more authors hold 13.7% together. The largest of them is Alec Thomas at 1.0%.

**Concentration**
- **Bus factor is 5.** I define it as the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines together reach 50% of the lines counted. Here that is Eric Paris, Noah Dietz, Alex Ogier and two more, who hold 51.0%.
- **To reach 80% takes 16 authors.** Both figures cover the whole Go code in the repository.
- **Losing one or two people:** The biggest single holder, Eric Paris, has 16.8%. The top two together have about 27%. That is a modest loss.
- **The real risk is inactivity.** Almost all of the top holders last committed between 2014 and 2019.
  - Only Georges Varouchas, Ethan P., Maximilian Frank, Lena Voss and Hu Jun have committed since 2025. Together they hold about 10% of the lines.
  - The people who know most of the code may not be around to ask.

**Caveats**
- **Duplicate names:** The same email appears under two names for three people. They are already counted as one person each:
  - bogem is also Albert Nigmatzianov (182 lines).
  - therealmitchconnors is also Mitch Connors (451 lines).
  - Charlie R.C is also xilabao (137 lines).
  - These are guesses from names and addresses. I did not write a `.mailmap` file because you only asked a question.
- **Mechanical rewrites:** I checked for them. The commit `6bf5b04` (`interface{}` → `any`, by Sam Okafor) rewrote 49 lines across 39 files and was not set aside automatically.
  - I re-ran with `--ignore-rev 6bf5b04`. Eric Paris moves to 1,802 lines (17.0%) and Noah Dietz to 1,084 (10.2%). The bus factor stays at 5, and 80% takes 15 authors.
  - Two more commits look mechanical and were kept: `d16d05e` (28 lines, bogem) and `92abf19` (1 line, bogem).
- **Copy detection:** The figures use `-w -M -C`. It moves 519 lines in 16 files to another author. Without `-C`, Alex Ogier would hold 7.1% (749 lines) instead of 10.1%. Riobard would hold 6.8% (722 lines) instead of 3.8%.
- **Gaps between lines and commits:** Several people hold many lines from one or two commits (Noah Dietz, Jonathan Reyna, Riobard). I did not check what those commits were, so they may be large imports or bulk additions. Check them before treating those people as owners.
- **What the numbers cannot show:** They say nothing about code quality, review work, design discussions, or code that was deleted on purpose.

**For your review work:** Run `--reviewers <path>` on the directory a PR touches to get a per-path reviewer suggestion. I didn't run it here.
